{
  "sufficient": true,
  "target_phase": "writing",
  "reason": "All four questions (including the optional weather one) are answered from real 2023-2024 Citi Bike data (79.41M raw trips, 79,371,599 kept after cleaning; 0.049% removed, each rule reported). I independently re-queried /app/parquet and reproduced the 24 monthly counts, the clean total, and the weekday/weekend peak hours by rider type; all match output/tables. Findings are backed by tables with bootstrap CIs and by figures. Remaining gaps are limitations to state in the report (unverified 2023 zip provenance, peak-month ambiguity, one-point weather proxy), not reasons for more experiments. I could not view figures myself (host copy was denied); the write-report job reported inspecting them, so the writer should confirm legibility.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "statement": "Monthly volume is strongly seasonal: lowest in winter (Jan/Feb), highest in a broad summer-autumn plateau. Peak/trough ratio is about 2.3 (2023) and 2.7 (2024) on monthly totals, and 2.2 (CI 2.07-2.42) and 2.7 (CI 2.49-3.14) per day. The exact peak month is not well pinned down (Aug 2023, Oct 2024 by totals; bootstrap agreement 55-88%). 2024 exceeds 2023 in every month (about 1.26x overall).",
      "status": "supported",
      "evidence": ["output/tables/monthly_counts.csv", "output/tables/seasonality_summary.csv", "output/tables/findings_numbers.json q1_seasonality", "my re-query: 2023 Feb 1,694,796 / Aug 3,961,140; 2024 Jan 1,887,308 / Oct 5,149,849", "output/figures/fig_monthly_volume.png"],
      "confidence": 0.93
    },
    {
      "statement": "Weekdays show two commute peaks, at 08:00 and 17:00, in both years (100% of bootstrap resamples agree). The 08:00 peak is about 1.5x the 11-14h midday mean and the 17:00 peak about 2.0x (2023: 1.46 and 1.97; 2024: 1.54 and 2.04). Excluding federal holidays shifts ratios by only 0.03-0.04. Weekends have a single broad midday/afternoon peak, centred at 15:00 (a plateau, 54-98% bootstrap agreement depending on year/season). Weekend days are less busy than weekdays on average, and Sunday is the lowest day of the week (about 0.85-0.88 of the weekly mean).",
      "status": "supported",
      "evidence": ["output/tables/peak_hours.csv", "output/tables/hourly_dow.csv", "output/tables/dow_daily_summary.csv", "output/figures/fig_hourly_weekday_vs_weekend.png", "output/figures/fig_heatmap_dow_hour.png", "my re-query of 2024 clean trips: member weekday top hours 17,18,8; weekend 15,14,13"],
      "confidence": 0.92
    },
    {
      "statement": "Members and casual riders differ. Casual riders take a larger share of trips on weekends (35.4% vs 23.3% for members in 2023, similar in 2024; CIs do not overlap). On weekdays members have both an 08:00 and a 17:00 peak, while casual riders have a weak or absent morning peak and a smaller evening peak. Weekday profile distance is moderate (Jensen-Shannon about 0.12), weekend profiles are nearly identical (about 0.06). Casual riding is much more seasonal (casual peak/trough about 4.4 in 2023 and 5.4 in 2024, vs about 2.1 and 2.5 for members). Casual trips are also longer (median about 13-14 min vs about 8.4-8.6 for members).",
      "status": "supported",
      "evidence": ["output/tables/member_casual_summary.csv", "output/tables/member_casual_profile_shape.csv", "output/tables/seasonality_summary.csv", "output/tables/findings_numbers.json", "output/figures/fig_member_vs_casual_hourly.png", "output/figures/fig_member_vs_casual_seasonal.png", "my re-query: casual weekday 2024 top hours 17,18,16 (08:00 not in top 3)"],
      "confidence": 0.88
    },
    {
      "statement": "Daily trip counts are positively associated with temperature (Spearman rho 0.73, block-bootstrap CI 0.62-0.78; casual 0.81, member 0.66) and negatively with precipitation (rho -0.29, CI -0.38 to -0.19). In an adjusted log model, relative to dry days trips are about 9% lower on 0-2.5 mm days, 23% lower on 2.5-10 mm, and 42% lower above 10 mm. The temperature effect is nonlinear (about +38% per +10C from 10 to 20C, about +11% from 20 to 30C). Within-month the temperature coefficient stays about +3.7%/C, so the association is not only shared seasonality, but season (daylight, school, tourism) cannot be fully separated. Association only, not causal.",
      "status": "supported",
      "evidence": ["output/tables/weather_numbers.json", "output/tables/weather_models.csv", "output/tables/weather_spearman.csv", "output/weather_section.md", "output/figures/fig_temp_vs_trips.png", "output/figures/fig_precip_vs_trips.png"],
      "confidence": 0.8
    },
    {
      "statement": "Data cleaning removed only 38,596 of 79,410,195 raw trips (0.049%; 27,230 in 2023, 11,366 in 2024). Dominant rule is duration > 24h (37,576). The 643 negative-duration rows are all on the two fall-back DST dates (artifact of repeated 01:00 hour), not bad data. Findings are insensitive to cleaning: dropping or keeping the sub-60s trips changes 182 rows.",
